From 84e5bc9460e9eed090046d7af8a1be7ae5233e6c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 16:06:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): a worker survives a long sleep with networkless
 DarkWakes [daveey]

Runs 10712/10714 (10-30, a MacBook asleep 16:57-21:31 with DNS-less
maintenance wakes) ended lease_lost failures although nobody else held them:

- Anthropic: every stale-stream kill counted toward the cross-turn stale
  breaker, but each kill only measured the suspend (watchdogs age on wall
  time); the fifth DarkWake gave the worker up. A kill whose
  wall-minus-monotonic gap grew past 30 s since the breaker last moved is now
  not counted; the excuse never carries over to later awake kills.
- Codex/any failed turn: in-place recovery refused at once when the claim
  lease had lapsed during the sleep (the renewing dispatcher was asleep too).
  worker_claim_state() now separates "lapsed" (every coordinate still ours,
  only the expiry passed) from "lost"; recovery waits read-only, bounded by
  300 awake seconds, for the dispatcher's holder-qualified extension. The
  worker never extends its own lease; a release, relock or takeover is
  "lost" and fences it out before model re-entry.
- The dispatcher's stale sweep ran before its crash sweep and booked a dead
  worker's own transient exit (75) as lease_lost. A host-local dead worker
  whose authority recorded exit 75 under this claim is now left to the same
  pass's crash sweep (neutral rate_limited requeue).

Regression: tests/agent/test_kanban_darkwake_recovery.py (13 of 15 RED on
the base, controls green; all GREEN here).
